-**Money paid over time is paid for real.** A tribute, a subsidy, a price paid
-over years ("twenty a month for ten years") is paid by the game itself: the
-first payment at once, then each time it falls due, from the treasury that
-owes it into the one it is owed to. What a treasury cannot pay is owed; owed
-for more than two months, the pact is broken. A trade in goods works the same
-way: the buyer pays the seller every month, and when the money stops, the
-goods stop.
-
-**The ruler's word.** Every promise you break and every deceit that comes to
-light (a lie, a forgery, a bribe that makes someone betray their master) marks
-your name. It weighs fully for ten years, then fades, and after fifty years it
-is forgotten; under a new ruler the old marks weigh less, and those you wronged
-remember their own wrong twice over. The more it is doubted, the more people
-want in hand before they give - an advance, a pledge, payment as each part is
-delivered - the less a promise of yours is worth to them, and the firmer they
-stand; with a worthless word, nothing is given on credit. Keeping promises to
-the end mends it slowly - never fully, and never in advance.
